#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046669} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552535} :activity/id #uuid "b0acf0c5-df93-5a94-be91-5f6d79d75ec4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048376 :topic/id #uuid "9a5670e8-43ef-54b7-8f4b-81842fc55f1c" :topic/math-academy-id 3715} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552533 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21a7742d-0ab1-5cb1-a4e0-9f04009f380a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551740} {:answer/id #uuid "39be7048-959a-5586-9a05-58dd72dd2a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551802} {:answer/id #uuid "4fdea056-e0b2-527a-a76b-3b7bd51c90d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-3{x}^{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551855} {:answer/id #uuid "63741c96-7347-5365-9dd4-5b7cb9ce2a0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy-x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551900} {:answer/id #uuid "71d55716-0405-5ae7-a22f-7c18c75cb125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551934}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551802} :answer-field/id #uuid "52e66d08-f648-5db7-b29b-0acb8e6ee730" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "832871bc-122c-548e-b34d-eed1097a268c" :question/math-academy-id "q-127052" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=3xy\\,i-{x}^{2}\\,j$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n=x\\,i+y\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/38/386f010932b5846e7f47a605a94a5b5d580bd35dbe37512b1583d15d29f3dc70.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(3xy\\,i-{x}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (xi+y\\,j)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}3{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}2{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $2{x}^{2}y.$"} :step/id #uuid "0a4580cf-0850-57eb-9f78-624b36e7297d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552605}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552535 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180037 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c270d16-d1b9-584c-a753-2613c93b2733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177892} {:answer/id #uuid "ac7ecac2-757d-5844-815f-910c23411410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177893} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2cb1ed-8729-57d0-bfa3-5b2591155680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177894} {:answer/id #uuid "0185a290-822d-58a0-ae4e-dfba4ef2a94a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177895} {:answer/id #uuid "0a7388ca-c74f-5074-90cf-b3f8cb2908d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177896}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177896} :answer-field/id #uuid "15b24aa2-07c6-5f2c-85f4-99a1708ccb2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2e3c5efb-b46d-57f5-8660-cecbe88198e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-126892" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4e47604540f3cfb3f73af34b0d3eb64bb649e9a42004d22eccc2dd9007de4fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=2x\\,i+y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(2x\\,i+y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=16.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(2x\\,i+y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+2\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=8.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 16+2\\cdot 8=48.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0bfd6bd6-d91d-5c9e-81a7-13d84f5ab582" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552533}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552567 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180042 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc34a4ce-679b-5695-9dfe-540cd60117ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177922} {:answer/id #uuid "e3fed570-9a0a-5fb6-b092-18090d3757e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177923} {:answer/id #uuid "034cd8e5-64a2-50b6-bec2-58e1146cb7e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177924} {:answer/id #uuid "64ad22d1-d74c-5598-8e39-3026099a604c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177925} {:answer/id #uuid "73d700d6-ddb9-5085-a028-fa38b1441baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177926}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177925} :answer-field/id #uuid "268bf099-9758-5e4a-bc30-751c0618a551" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "30107d9c-6bc8-5ab5-a34a-0024b1e426f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-127199" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5c8aef7238da3bb5aed0fd2a458f6fd9bcd42884bbd350616e58e7a25b1f0fe2.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-2x\\,i-2y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/87/878a9992405e5968a03a04d12f42aeb85bca52c07dc7081244b04011643a74bf.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-2x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-2{\\int }_{-3}^{3}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6(3-(-3)) \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot (-36)=-144.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "730a67bf-8e4a-5cfd-bae7-d76380de5932"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552601 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537939 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1add2c2e-a167-5c2c-b545-a5f1eb517140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537284} {:answer/id #uuid "28b45251-3b7f-5715-8789-20bd83838a64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537309} {:answer/id #uuid "91c49acb-1fa6-5ddc-9050-3b83a7f4c764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537504} {:answer/id #uuid "ba10258f-f51b-5e95-9c08-2bf569bcb880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537591} {:answer/id #uuid "d3235a30-a18e-50b4-a05b-6c1309b09270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537643}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537284} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bc18207-a6fd-5437-a68d-b3d6fef6d67e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "90bd3eab-412f-52dc-83f5-2f8877931db1" :question/math-academy-id "q-127193" :question/problem "![](images/32/32d4722f4a1dd1cfae3fb22cde7399dcb66623e484bcd5989fd2de246a905b51.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-3\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=-3\\,j$ is parallel to ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net inflow through ${C}_{2}$ will cancel with the net outflow through ${C}_{4}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c89c7176-5d6b-5363-9c6e-85aaf0539a59" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552567}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552605 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ac0a72f-211e-5ee3-ae1a-cdee72cf08a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551763} {:answer/id #uuid "2d182642-d007-5fcb-afa9-a8fa7ef431cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551773} {:answer/id #uuid "5e5d59b2-0373-5c0b-afdf-10bb5a2f8068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551891} {:answer/id #uuid "728d6f69-9ad5-569f-bfa9-38eefd7f9277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551938} {:answer/id #uuid "74cf75dc-c6e4-5de4-a02f-72578e740790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551941}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551941} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1dca841-c306-5d01-962c-2f2e21f3e369" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "89cfc1e4-7ca7-548a-8985-27acedeb025d" :question/math-academy-id "q-127054" :question/problem "![](images/0d/0dd48f19e9940f1684c0cbb723e772384dda3d7429fc89c2c9dc528f7ebfe42c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)=xy\\,i-{x}^{2}y\\,j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{4}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{4}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $y$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=-j.$\n\n![](images/c5/c591fedf1b1c8ea705f0c13fbfd96bc7f1f6701841e3eff03bd0578aa2f8ab06.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}(xy\\,i-{x}^{2}y\\,j)\\cdot (-j)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{2}y.$"} :step/id #uuid "ccdf0133-78bd-5793-ad8e-1b987169a976" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552601}}] :activity/title "Flux in Two-Dimensional Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551659}]}
